------ Deferred from the Plotly chart + filters rework (pitching page, phase 1) ------
Phases still to do, in order:
  2. DONE -- pitch-by-pitch at-bat panels are native ReportLab vector drawings (services/ab_chart.py),
     for both the pitcher and hitter PDFs; no matplotlib, no image files, no process pool for the bulk download
  3. DONE -- hitter + catcher pages: same filter bar, Plotly charts, client-rendered PDF images; no
     worker pool is left anywhere (shared card controller in static/js/report_cards.js)
  4. DONE -- custom report contract: get_charts(source, id) returns CustomChart(title, Plotly figure) instead
     of PNG paths; school 2's custom_hitter_report.py rewritten; matplotlib + seaborn removed from
     requirements.txt and from report_theme.py (nothing in the app imports either any more)
Accepted risks / known limits:
  - PDF chart images are client-supplied. chart_images.save_chart_image validates and re-encodes them
    (PNG only, size/dimension caps, fixed slot names), but a user can put any picture in their OWN
    PDF's chart slots. Numbers and tables are always recomputed server-side.
  - plotly.js loads from cdn.plot.ly with no SRI hash. If a school network blocks the CDN the
    charts (and PDF chart images) are unavailable; tables still work. Vendor it into /static if
    that ever shows up.
  - game_archive.load_games_cached keeps the last 2 selections' DataFrames in memory per worker
    (shared across requests -- callers must treat them as read-only). Gunicorn recycles workers
    every ~20 requests, which bounds this.
  - A pitch with no tracked velocity is dropped once a velocity range is set (can't show it
    falls in range); it is kept, in gray, when coloring by velocity with no range set.
  - Filters also narrow the pitch-by-pitch PDFs (at-bat sequences lose the filtered-out pitches).
  - Heatmaps are a numpy Gaussian KDE evaluated over the plot's own limits, so they look close to
    the old seaborn ones but not pixel-identical (contour levels differ).
  - Every filter/chart-mode change re-runs build_table etc. for each OPEN card; closed cards refetch
    when opened or exported. Fine at roster scale; revisit if a selection spans many games.
  - Hitter pages: the velocity slider filters the pitch velocity the hitter saw, but the "velocity" chart
    color on a spray chart is the batted ball's EXIT velocity (quartile scale from his unfiltered exit speeds).
  - Catcher page: color defaults to framing value regardless of the account's pitch-type/velocity setting
    (a framing chart is for framing); the page's color control is a dropdown since it has three choices.
  - DEPLOY: a school's custom_hitter_report.py lives in that school's assets (the Railway volume, not this repo
    for schools other than the ones checked in). Any copy still using the old get_charts(source, id, user_id,
    output_dir) contract or importing matplotlib must be re-uploaded before this ships: a script that imports
    matplotlib now fails to load, which drops that school's custom hitter section (stats and tables too), and
    one that only has the old get_charts signature just loses its charts (logged, never an error on the page).
    School assets are gitignored (the rewritten school 2 script exists only in app/storage/schools/2/assets/ on
    this machine); docs/examples/custom_hitter_chart.py is the tracked worked example of the new contract.
  - Dockerfile still apt-installs libcairo2/libpango/libgdk-pixbuf; nothing in the repo uses them. Probably
    left over from before ReportLab and safe to drop to shrink the image -- verify on a Railway build first.
  - A heatmap needs HEATMAP_MIN_PITCHES (8) points per group; a hitter with fewer batted balls, or a catcher
    with few taken pitches, silently shows points in heatmap view.

------ Deferred from the memory audit ------
Measured (real school archives, Windows dev box): the process sits at ~150 MB right after
  imports (pandas+numpy ~58, flask/sqlalchemy/migrate ~43, pypdf ~10, openpyxl ~7, reportlab ~6);
  loading a school's whole archive (8 games) adds ~6 MB and 12 pitcher charts ~5 MB. Request-time
  data is NOT the cost driver at current archive sizes -- the always-on baseline is. Re-measure with
  `python scripts/measure_memory.py` and MEMORY_LOG=1 before spending effort on dtypes/usecols.
Railway confirmed (7 days): memory flat at ~150 MB, never below 100 -- matches the import baseline, so
  the cost is the always-on process, not requests or growth. Levers left: app sleeping (idle time),
  lazy-importing reportlab/openpyxl/alembic (~10-20 MB, reclaimed only until first use).
Dtype/usecols loader (category dtypes, float32, usecols with custom scripts declaring
  REQUIRED_COLUMNS) -- deferred: wins only matter once archives reach hundreds of games.
Export PDF is read fully into BytesIO before the work dir is deleted (pitching/batting/catching
  export routes) -- fine for one report, transient tens of MB for a 100-player merge. Accepted.
--max-requests 20 in the Dockerfile recycles the worker; it may be masking growth rather than
  fixing it. Revisit once a Railway memory graph shows whether the baseline climbs between recycles.
